The approach is to count how often each character appears, then check that every non-zero count equals the first one.

The examples show a trailing space inside the quotes ("abacbc "), which is probably a formatting artifact. If the space were really part of the input, it would be counted as a character like any other. In Example 1 that would give a space count of 1, and the expected output of true would then be wrong. So the space is an artifact and I count characters as given.

```javascript
/**
 * @param {string} s
 * @return {boolean}
 */
var areOccurrencesEqual = function(s) {
    const m = new Map();
    for (const c of s) m.set(c, (m.get(c) || 0) + 1);
    let f = -1;
    for (const v of m.values()) {
        if (f === -1) f = v;
        else if (f !== v) return false;
    }
    return true;
};
```

