traces.inference_trace:
Sort the array and let dp[i] be the number of trees whose root is arr[i]. Then dp[i] = 1 + sum over pairs (a, b) with a·b = arr[i] of dp[a]·dp[b]. A map from value to dp gives O(1) lookups for the cofactor.

Details:
- Values go up to 1e9, so the product a·b can reach about 1e18. I avoid computing products and use division instead: for each j < i with arr[i] % arr[j] === 0, set the cofactor q = arr[i] / arr[j] and look it up in the map.
- dp values are below 1e9+7, so their product can exceed 2^53 and needs BigInt, or a modular multiplication split into parts. I'll use BigInt for the multiplication only: `Number(BigInt(x) * BigInt(y) % MOD)`. With about 500k pairs at most, this is fast enough.
- Sorting must be numeric: `arr.sort((a, b) => a - b)`.
- Each ordered pair (j, q) is counted separately as j iterates over all smaller elements, so the left/right order is handled automatically. For example, 10 = 2·5 and 10 = 5·2 are both counted, which matches example 2.

```javascript
/**
 * @param {number[]} arr
 * @return {number}
 */
var numFactoredBinaryTrees = function(arr) {
    const MOD = 1000000007n;
    arr = arr.slice().sort((a, b) => a - b);
    const n = arr.length;
    const map = new Map();
    let total = 0n;
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        let cur = 1n;
        const v = arr[i];
        for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
            const a = arr[j];
            if (v % a === 0) {
                const q = v / a;
                const dq = map.get(q);
                if (dq !== undefined) {
                    cur = (cur + map.get(a) * dq) % MOD;
                }
            }
        }
        map.set(v, cur);
        total = (total + cur) % MOD;
    }
    return Number(total);
};
```

