This uses patience sorting with binary search, which runs in O(n log n). The array `tails` holds, at index `i`, the smallest possible tail value of any strictly increasing subsequence of length `i+1`.

For each number, binary search for the first tail that is greater than or equal to it (lower bound). If one exists, replace it with the number. Otherwise, append the number. The length of `tails` at the end is the answer.

Using lower bound (`>=`) is what keeps the subsequence strictly increasing, so equal values replace an existing tail instead of extending the sequence.

```javascript
/**
 * @param {number[]} nums
 * @return {number}
