The approach is to remove every initial node in turn and simulate the spread. With n ≤ 300, a BFS over the adjacency matrix costs O(n²) per removal, so the total is O(k·n²) ≤ 27M operations, which is fine.

Steps:
1. For each node `r` in `initial`, mark `r` as removed so it is never visited and its edges are ignored.
2. Start a BFS from all other initial nodes and count the infected nodes.
3. Keep the node with the smallest resulting count. On ties, keep the smaller index.

The signature must stay as given, so the function is `minMalwareSpread` and it takes `graph` and `initial`.

```javascript
/**
 * @param {number[][]} graph
 * @param {number[]} initial
 * @return {number}
 */
var minMalwareSpread = function(graph, initial) {
    const n = graph.length;
    let best = -1, bestCount = Infinity;
    for (const r of initial) {
        const visited = new Uint8Array(n);
        visited[r] = 1;
        const queue = [];
        let count = 0;
        for (const s of initial) {
            if (s !== r && !visited[s]) {
                visited[s] = 1;
                queue.push(s);
                count++;
            }
        }
        let head = 0;
        while (head < queue.length) {
            const u = queue[head++];
            const row = graph[u];
            for (let v = 0; v < n; v++) {
                if (row[v] === 1 && !visited[v]) {
                    visited[v] = 1;
                    count++;
                    queue.push(v);
                }
            }
        }
        if (count < bestCount || (count === bestCount && r < best)) {
            bestCount = count;
            best = r;
        }
    }
    return best;
};
```

