This uses bottom-up dynamic programming. Let `dp[i][j]` be true when `s[i..]` matches `p[j..]`.

- Base case: `dp[m][n] = true`, since an empty string matches an empty pattern.
- Fill `i` from `m` down to 0 and `j` from `n-1` down to 0.
- Let `first = i < m && (p[j] === s[i] || p[j] === '.')`.
- If `p[j+1] === '*'`, then `dp[i][j] = dp[i][j+2] || (first && dp[i+1][j])`. The first option uses zero copies of the preceding element, and the second consumes one character and stays on the same pattern position.
- Otherwise, `dp[i][j] = first && dp[i+1][j+1]`.

