Approach: sort the products, then for each prefix of `searchWord`, narrow a window `[lo, hi]` of the sorted array. At each character `i`, advance `lo` while the product at `lo` is too short or its character at `i` differs from `searchWord[i]`. Likewise decrease `hi` from the other end. Then take up to three products from `lo` to `hi`.

The window shrinks correctly because the products inside it all share the prefix `searchWord[0..i-1]`, and sorted order keeps the matching ones contiguous. The check `products[lo][i] !== c` also covers a product that is too short, since `undefined !== c`.

Sorting uses the default JS sort, which compares by UTF-16 code units. That is fine for lowercase letters.

The work is O(n log n) for the sort plus O(n + m) pointer moves.

```javascript
