This is a shortest-path problem on an implicit graph, so I use BFS from `beginWord`. Two words are neighbors if they differ by one letter.

1. Put `wordList` in a set. If `endWord` is not in it, return 0.
2. Start the queue with `beginWord` at distance 1.
3. For each word taken from the queue, try replacing every position with each of the letters `a` to `z`.
4. If a new word is in the set, remove it from the set so it is never visited again, and enqueue it with distance + 1. If that word is `endWord`, return its distance.
5. If the queue empties without reaching `endWord`, return 0.

Cost: up to 5000 words × 10 positions × 26 letters is about 1.3M candidate checks, which is fine.

The statement's examples show trailing spaces inside the strings (an artifact of the formatting), but the constraints say lowercase letters only, so I treat the words as plain lowercase strings. I use an index pointer on an array instead of `shift()` to keep the queue O(1) per operation.

```javascript
var ladderLength = function(beginWord, endWord, wordList) {
    const set = new Set(wordList);
    if (!set.has(endWord)) return 0;
    let queue = [beginWord];
    let dist = [1];
    let head = 0;
    while (head < queue.length) {
        const w = queue[head];
        const d = dist[head];
        head++;
        for (let i = 0; i < w.length; i++) {
            for (let c = 97; c < 123; c++) {
                const ch = String.fromCharCode(c);
                if (ch === w[i]) continue;
                const nw = w.slice(0, i) + ch + w.slice(i + 1);
                if (set.has(nw)) {
                    if (nw === endWord) return d + 1;
                    set.delete(nw);
                    queue.push(nw);
                    dist.push(d + 1);
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return 0;
};
```
